WEBVTT

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:07.337
Captain's Log, Stardate 48532.4.

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:09.433
We're on our way
to a rogue planetoid

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:10.704
which Mr. Neelix tells us

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:13.440
is an extremely rich source
of raw dilithium.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.641
If he's right,
this could go a long way

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:18.515
toward easing
our power shortage.

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:21.645
Assuming we do find dilithium
on this planetoid,

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.315
we're going to need
a refining facility

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:24.880
on the ship to process it.

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:26.653
Lieutenant Torres
has already asked permission

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:27.819
to start modifications

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:30.352
to the auxiliary
impulse reactor.

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:34.163
It could be converted into
a crude dilithium refinery.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.327
The impulse reactor?

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:38.462
Sometimes I think B'Elanna
goes out of her way

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:41.235
to find solutions that ignore
Starfleet procedures.

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:42.707
Her arguments
are quite convincing.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:44.369
She thinks
it can be done safely.

00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:45.503
I'm sure it can.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:48.013
Tell her I want regular
reports on her progress

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:50.015
every step of the way.

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:51.948
Are you sure you won't
join me for breakfast?

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:55.978
I was thinking of having
eggs Benedict with asparagus,

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:58.718
strawberries and cream...

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:02.861
I said I was thinking about it.

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:05.626
I'm actually having
ration pack #5...

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.929
stewed tomatoes
with dehydrated eggs.

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.901
Mmm, sounds delicious,
but I've already had

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.870
my vacuum-packed oatmeal
this morning.

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:13.270
I'll see you on the Bridge.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.651
Here you are.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:29.916
Hope you enjoy it.

00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:31.184
What is going on here?

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:32.816
Captain, you caught
me by surprise.

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:33.985
I could say the same thing.

00:01:34.094 --> 00:01:35.493
What are you doing?

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:38.759
I know how you and the
other, uh, senior officers

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:39.924
have been... excuse me...

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:41.627
disappointed
with the rations lately,

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:44.193
so I thought I'd use
a few of the vegetables

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:46.933
from the hydroponics
bay and whip up a...

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:48.304
ooh, a little breakfast.

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:50.832
You have turned this
into a Galley?

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:52.105
It wasn't easy.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:55.012
I had to completely reroute
the Mess Hall power conduits

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:57.778
and scrounge a lot of supplies
from all over the ship,

00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:01.583
but that's my specialty...
making something out of nothing.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:02.952
I know it doesn't
look like much now,

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.489
but in a few days, you'll swear

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:06.321
there'd been a Galley here
for years.

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:08.054
Careful.
That one is a little spicy.

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:10.358
Neelix... who approved this?

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.990
Uh, well... no one.

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:18.863
Well, you might have
asked me first.

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.669
This used to be
my private dining room.

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:23.539
Your, your dining room?

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:25.066
If you had checked
the ship's directory,

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:28.080
you'd have noticed
that Cabin 125-Alpha, Deck 2,

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.639
is designated as the Captain's
Private Dining Room.

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:35.620
Oh... oh, ah, then I guess
that you'll be wanting me

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:37.885
to move all of this stuff
out of your way?

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:39.118
Bridge to Captain Janeway.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:40.251
Go ahead.

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:41.885
We're approaching
the rogue planetoid, Captain.

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:43.156
On my way.

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:44.424
I want you to come with me.

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.830
We will deal with this later.

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:49.300
Ensign Parsons, would you mind

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.098
keeping an eye on things
until I get back...

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:54.629
and, uh, rotate the darvot
fritters every ten minutes

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:56.335
until they turn
a deep chartreuse.

00:02:56.376 --> 00:02:57.900
And remember... one,
one to a customer.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:03.243
What have you found?

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:05.148
We're picking up definite
dilithium signatures, Captain.

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:08.916
The strongest readings
are originating from ten to 20

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:10.946
kilometers inside the planetoid.

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:13.150
It also looks like there's
a series of subterranean caves

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:15.753
with an oxygen-nitrogen
atmosphere.

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:17.956
Class-M.
It'll make mining a lot easier

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.828
if we can go in there
without environmental suits.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.391
How much dilithium
are we talking about?

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:24.596
It's hard to get
a precise reading.

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:28.630
It could be anywhere from 500
to... 1,000 metric tons.

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:30.404
Just as I said.

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:32.968
I bet there are
few Yallitian engineers

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.912
who'd give all three
of their spinal columns

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:37.212
to know where this planet is.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.378
Commander, take an away team
down into those caves and do

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:41.819
a preliminary
geological analysis.

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:42.980
Lieutenant, you'd better get

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:45.252
your dilithium refinery on line.

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:46.986
It'll be ready to go
in three days.

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:48.653
Ensign Kim.

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:54.124
Where are you going?

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:55.191
With you.

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:58.500
I've been studying
my Tricorder Operations Manual.

00:03:58.538 --> 00:04:00.199
Lieutenant Torres
has brought me up-to-date

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.730
on dilithium geophysics.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:05.676
I've been preparing
for this mission all week.

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:10.381
Very well, Mr. Neelix.

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:14.182
I think you'll find me
extremely helpful, Commander.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.780
I remember the first time
that I flew past this planet.

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:17.949
It was about three years ago...

00:04:17.991 --> 00:04:19.083
Deck 4.

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:23.919
Looks like there are several
large deposits

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.157
in this general vicinity.

00:04:25.198 --> 00:04:28.531
Let's split up and begin
taking geological scans.

00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:30.968
Keep your com channels
open at all times

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:32.835
and don't wander too far.

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:35.534
Stay within a 50-meter
radius of this position.

00:06:46.339 --> 00:06:47.306
Chakotay to Kim.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.307
Go ahead.

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:50.707
I'm still picking up
dilithium signatures...

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:55.146
but there are no
dilithium formations here.

00:06:55.181 --> 00:06:57.274
Have you found anything yet?

00:06:57.316 --> 00:06:58.544
I'm running
a geo-stratal analysis

00:06:58.584 --> 00:06:59.710
of the rock in this vicinity.

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:02.585
But so far, there's no
indication of any dilithium.

00:07:02.622 --> 00:07:04.590
Keep looking.

00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:06.819
Neelix to Commander Chakotay.

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:07.917
Go ahead.

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:12.794
I think I found something...

00:07:12.832 --> 00:07:19.704
a large cavern approximately
20 meters from my position.

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:23.539
I'm reading a huge
dilithium formation there.

00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:24.943
Don't go any further, Neelix.

00:07:25.076 --> 00:07:27.601
Stay within the search radius.

00:07:27.645 --> 00:07:29.272
I'll be with you in a minute.

00:07:29.314 --> 00:07:30.804
But it's right here!

00:07:33.484 --> 00:07:36.647
I'm in the cavern now,
Commander, but...

00:07:37.989 --> 00:07:39.854
I don't understand this.

00:07:39.891 --> 00:07:42.451
Let me guess... nothing there?

00:07:42.493 --> 00:07:45.860
Not so much as a sliver
of dilithium.

00:07:45.897 --> 00:07:47.728
And yet, according
to my readings,

00:07:47.765 --> 00:07:49.494
I should be surrounded by it.

00:07:49.534 --> 00:07:50.796
All right, I've had enough
of this.

00:07:50.835 --> 00:07:54.066
Kim, Neelix,
we're heading back to the ship.

00:07:54.105 --> 00:07:55.163
Rendezvous with the...

00:07:55.206 --> 00:07:56.366
Wait a minute.

00:07:56.407 --> 00:08:00.434
I'm getting unusual readings
from this rock face.

00:08:00.478 --> 00:08:03.379
Traces of organic energy.

00:08:03.414 --> 00:08:07.510
Looks like
a bioelectrical signature.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.385
I think there's something alive
down here.

00:08:14.158 --> 00:08:18.925
It's about... two meters
into this rock face.

00:08:18.997 --> 00:08:21.124
Neelix, I said get back here.

00:08:26.938 --> 00:08:29.236
Neelix, did you hear
what I said?

00:08:29.273 --> 00:08:31.764
Just one minute, Commander.

00:08:41.819 --> 00:08:42.786
Hello?

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:46.256
Is there someone there?

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:48.155
Hello?

00:08:50.595 --> 00:08:52.062
I know there's somebody...

00:08:56.234 --> 00:08:57.895
Neelix, what's wrong?

00:08:59.303 --> 00:09:01.897
Neelix!

00:09:01.939 --> 00:09:03.031
Kim!

00:09:03.074 --> 00:09:04.871
I'm on my way, sir.

00:09:07.378 --> 00:09:09.812
I can't be sure, but I think
he's going into shock.

00:09:09.847 --> 00:09:12.441
Chakotay to Voyager...
medical emergency.

00:09:12.483 --> 00:09:14.348
Beam us directly to Sick Bay.

00:09:25.563 --> 00:09:27.224
Get the blood-gas infuser.

00:09:27.265 --> 00:09:28.459
The what?

00:09:28.499 --> 00:09:31.525
Equipment Storage Unit 2,
second shelf on the left, now.

00:09:31.569 --> 00:09:33.594
Hold him still.

00:09:36.407 --> 00:09:37.635
What's happening, Doctor?

00:09:37.675 --> 00:09:39.438
He's going into a coma.

00:09:41.245 --> 00:09:43.611
The infuser will keep
his oxygen level stable

00:09:43.648 --> 00:09:46.412
for the next hour or so,
but after that, he'll die.

00:09:46.451 --> 00:09:48.578
His lungs have been removed.

00:09:54.025 --> 00:09:56.289
By the time we got to him,
he was unconscious.

00:09:56.327 --> 00:09:58.761
How could anyone remove
his organs so quickly?

00:09:58.796 --> 00:09:59.956
The doctor says
whoever did this

00:10:00.064 --> 00:10:01.190
used some kind of transporter

00:10:01.232 --> 00:10:03.757
to beam the lungs
directly out of his body.

00:10:03.801 --> 00:10:05.234
What happened?

00:10:05.269 --> 00:10:08.067
It appears that Neelix
was attacked.

00:10:08.106 --> 00:10:09.266
We don't know how it happened,

00:10:09.307 --> 00:10:13.175
but someone has surgically
removed his lungs.

00:10:15.079 --> 00:10:16.273
Did you find any evidence

00:10:16.314 --> 00:10:17.611
of the life sign
that Neelix reported?

00:10:17.648 --> 00:10:18.615
No.

00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:20.412
Captain, I've analyzed
the sensor logs

00:10:20.451 --> 00:10:21.611
from Neelix's tricorder.

00:10:21.652 --> 00:10:22.778
The bio-scanner picked up

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:25.084
a single class-3
humanoid organism.

00:10:25.123 --> 00:10:26.215
The blood-gas infuser

00:10:26.257 --> 00:10:28.555
will keep him alive
for another 47 minutes.

00:10:28.593 --> 00:10:31.289
The only chance for his survival
I see at the moment

00:10:31.329 --> 00:10:32.853
is to get his own lungs back.

00:10:32.897 --> 00:10:35.491
Can't we fit him with
a pair of artificial lungs?

00:10:35.533 --> 00:10:37.296
His respiratory system
is directly linked

00:10:37.335 --> 00:10:39.530
to multiple points
along his spinal column.

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:41.094
It's too complex to replicate.

00:10:41.139 --> 00:10:43.198
I may be able to surgically
reattach the organs

00:10:43.241 --> 00:10:44.572
if we get them back.

00:10:44.609 --> 00:10:47.339
In the meantime, I'll have
to search for other options.

00:10:47.378 --> 00:10:51.280
Commander, I'm taking an away
team back to the planet.

00:10:51.315 --> 00:10:53.215
You're in charge
until we return.

00:10:53.251 --> 00:10:54.548
Inform Mr. Tuvok

00:10:54.585 --> 00:10:56.883
I want three armed security
detachments to accompany us.

00:10:56.921 --> 00:10:58.320
Issue type-3 phasers.

00:10:58.356 --> 00:10:59.584
Mr. Paris,

00:10:59.624 --> 00:11:01.387
did they teach you how to
run a respiratory series

00:11:01.425 --> 00:11:04.189
in your biochemistry
classes at the Academy?

00:11:04.228 --> 00:11:06.162
No, I'm afraid they didn't.

00:11:06.197 --> 00:11:09.257
Fine. I'll run it myself.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.268
Get me a pulmonary scanner.

00:11:11.302 --> 00:11:13.429
I'm going to have
to ask you to leave.

00:11:13.471 --> 00:11:14.665
I'm not leaving.

00:11:14.705 --> 00:11:17.196
All right, just keep
out of the way.

00:11:21.145 --> 00:11:22.112
Here it is.

00:11:22.146 --> 00:11:23.477
This is where Neelix
was standing

00:11:23.514 --> 00:11:24.481
when he was attacked.

00:11:24.515 --> 00:11:25.743
According to his tricorder log,

00:11:25.783 --> 00:11:28.308
he was scanning this rock face
when he detected the life sign.

00:11:28.352 --> 00:11:29.785
Neelix said there was
a life sign

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:32.152
two meters behind this wall,

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:35.819
but my tricorder says
there's nothing but solid rock

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:37.987
for another 15 meters.

00:11:38.062 --> 00:11:39.222
It sounds like our tricorders

00:11:39.263 --> 00:11:40.525
aren't giving us
the real picture

00:11:40.565 --> 00:11:41.623
behind what's going on here.

00:11:41.666 --> 00:11:43.224
Wait a minute.

00:11:43.267 --> 00:11:45.827
The rock is warmer here.

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:46.837
You're right, Captain.

00:11:46.871 --> 00:11:47.997
There's a two-degree
differential

00:11:48.105 --> 00:11:49.197
in this section of the wall.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.208
There are no natural
geological phenomena

00:11:51.242 --> 00:11:53.073
that could be creating
this heat source.

00:11:54.078 --> 00:11:56.410
Let's make a little heat
of our own.

00:12:02.954 --> 00:12:05.889
There was a highly sophisticated
force field in place.

00:12:05.923 --> 00:12:08.255
There's a chamber
down this corridor.

00:12:17.001 --> 00:12:22.132
Doc, I think his cellular
toxicity level is rising.

00:12:23.407 --> 00:12:25.068
It's up to 32 percent.

00:12:25.109 --> 00:12:26.838
Let's see if we can
stabilize those levels.

00:12:26.877 --> 00:12:28.367
Get me a cytoplasmic
stimulator.

00:12:29.647 --> 00:12:31.808
Ah, we don't have one.

00:12:31.849 --> 00:12:33.476
Then replicate one.

00:12:33.517 --> 00:12:34.711
Yeah.

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:41.520
The design schematics are in
the ship's medical data base.

00:12:42.827 --> 00:12:43.919
Right.

00:12:43.961 --> 00:12:46.953
The man drives
a 700,000-ton starship

00:12:46.998 --> 00:12:49.091
so somebody thinks
he'd make a good medic.

00:12:49.133 --> 00:12:50.896
I can survive with
one lung, can't I?

00:12:50.935 --> 00:12:53.096
What if I donated
a lung to Neelix?

00:12:53.137 --> 00:12:54.729
A transplant is not an option.

00:12:54.772 --> 00:12:57.332
No one aboard is a compatible
match for a Talaxian.

00:12:57.375 --> 00:12:59.775
We're going to need
a completely new way

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:01.539
to oxygenate his blood supply

00:13:01.579 --> 00:13:03.979
and relay
neuro-electrical impulses.

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.820
Our replicators can't produce

00:13:11.856 --> 00:13:13.414
compatible artificial
organs for him,

00:13:13.457 --> 00:13:17.325
but maybe there's a way
to mimic their functions

00:13:17.361 --> 00:13:20.819
and give us direct control
of his respiratory system.

00:13:20.865 --> 00:13:23.732
Computer, is there
an identification matrix

00:13:23.768 --> 00:13:25.668
on Mr. Neelix from the last
time he used the transporter?

00:13:25.703 --> 00:13:26.670
Affirmative.

00:13:26.704 --> 00:13:28.103
Transfer it to console 4.

00:13:31.775 --> 00:13:32.935
What are you doing?

00:13:32.976 --> 00:13:34.500
I'm using the transporter matrix

00:13:34.544 --> 00:13:36.978
to get exact specifications
for Neelix's lungs.

00:13:37.014 --> 00:13:39.448
I thought you just said
we can't replicate his lungs.

00:13:39.483 --> 00:13:43.977
We can't, but if I can
reconfigure my emitter array,

00:13:44.054 --> 00:13:46.852
I might be able to create
a pair of holographic lungs

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:48.084
for Mr. Neelix.

00:13:48.125 --> 00:13:50.650
Holographic lungs?

00:13:50.694 --> 00:13:51.661
If it's successful,

00:13:51.695 --> 00:13:53.720
we can precisely control
his pulmonary functions

00:13:53.764 --> 00:13:55.129
to allow normal breathing.

00:13:58.402 --> 00:14:00.427
But a hologram is just
a projection of light

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:02.199
held in a magnetic
containment field.

00:14:02.239 --> 00:14:03.763
There's no real matter involved.

00:14:04.741 --> 00:14:05.730
Now...

00:14:08.946 --> 00:14:10.709
you hit me.

00:14:14.284 --> 00:14:15.717
The magnetic containment field

00:14:15.752 --> 00:14:17.515
that creates the illusion
of my body

00:14:17.554 --> 00:14:20.352
can be modulated to allow
matter to pass through it

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:24.156
or... be stopped.

00:14:24.194 --> 00:14:26.094
I might be able to modulate
the holographic lungs

00:14:26.129 --> 00:14:27.096
in the same way,

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:28.688
allowing oxygen
and carbon dioxide

00:14:28.732 --> 00:14:30.723
to pass from the lungs
to the bloodstream.

00:14:30.767 --> 00:14:32.860
I want to know what this means.

00:14:32.903 --> 00:14:34.370
What are you going to do to him?

00:14:34.404 --> 00:14:35.371
There's no time to explain

00:14:35.405 --> 00:14:36.633
the exact procedure
to you right now.

00:14:36.673 --> 00:14:38.300
Oh, well, make the time,

00:14:38.342 --> 00:14:40.105
because I'm not going to let you

00:14:40.143 --> 00:14:42.509
perform any experimental
surgery on Neelix

00:14:42.546 --> 00:14:44.537
until I know exactly
what you're doing

00:14:44.581 --> 00:14:45.843
and what the risks are.

00:14:48.151 --> 00:14:50.517
The risk is that it won't work.

00:14:50.554 --> 00:14:52.112
And if it doesn't, he'll die.

00:14:52.155 --> 00:14:55.215
But it is his only
immediate chance for survival.

00:14:55.258 --> 00:14:56.418
If he does survive,

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.691
he'll have to be held motionless
in an isotropic restraint.

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:01.322
The lungs need to be
perfectly aligned

00:15:01.365 --> 00:15:03.265
to his internal physiology.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:05.029
The computer won't
be able to compensate

00:15:05.068 --> 00:15:06.501
for any body movement
whatsoever.

00:15:06.536 --> 00:15:09.061
How long will he have
to stay like that?

00:15:09.106 --> 00:15:11.131
For the rest of his life,

00:15:11.174 --> 00:15:14.166
unless the holo-lungs can be
replaced by his original organs,

00:15:14.211 --> 00:15:15.940
and he will never be able
to leave

00:15:16.013 --> 00:15:18.208
the holographic environment
of this room.

00:15:18.248 --> 00:15:19.545
The holo-lungs would disappear

00:15:19.583 --> 00:15:20.982
the moment he went out the door.

00:15:27.891 --> 00:15:32.555
Kes, I know it sounds
pretty grim,

00:15:32.596 --> 00:15:35.326
but it's better than
losing him completely.

00:15:35.365 --> 00:15:38.334
This way, he'll still be alive.

00:15:38.368 --> 00:15:40.598
And maybe we can find
a better solution

00:15:40.637 --> 00:15:42.070
somewhere down the road.

00:15:47.244 --> 00:15:49.610
What do you think
Neelix would want?

00:15:49.646 --> 00:15:52.308
I'm not sure.

00:15:52.349 --> 00:15:56.615
But I do know
that I want him to live.

00:16:31.021 --> 00:16:33.489
This room is the source
of the dilithium signature

00:16:33.523 --> 00:16:34.922
we detected earlier.

00:16:34.958 --> 00:16:36.084
The power systems here

00:16:36.126 --> 00:16:39.061
are running on an unusual
dilithium matrix.

00:16:39.096 --> 00:16:40.563
So there are actually
no dilithium deposits

00:16:40.597 --> 00:16:41.723
on this planet?

00:16:41.765 --> 00:16:43.289
It would appear not.

00:16:46.736 --> 00:16:48.636
Are these organs?

00:16:49.739 --> 00:16:52.469
This looks like some kind
of storage facility.

00:16:52.509 --> 00:16:54.477
I would say
you are correct, Captain.

00:16:54.511 --> 00:16:57.071
This appears to be
a biological repository.

00:16:57.114 --> 00:16:59.674
Any sign of Neelix's lungs?

00:16:59.716 --> 00:17:00.944
Negative.

00:17:00.984 --> 00:17:03.145
There is, however,
a Kazon liver,

00:17:03.186 --> 00:17:04.380
and although I cannot identify

00:17:04.421 --> 00:17:06.321
the other organs
on display here,

00:17:06.356 --> 00:17:07.846
I would say they bear
a striking similarity

00:17:07.891 --> 00:17:10.257
to pulmonary organs,
epidermal tissue,

00:17:10.293 --> 00:17:11.260
ocular nerve fiber...

00:17:11.294 --> 00:17:13.125
Save the cataloging
for later, Tuvok.

00:17:13.163 --> 00:17:14.425
According to my tricorder,

00:17:14.464 --> 00:17:16.557
there was a life form in this
room less than ten minutes ago.

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:17.567
Captain.

00:17:17.601 --> 00:17:18.795
Open it.

00:17:23.073 --> 00:17:24.597
One life sign 20 meters ahead.

00:17:24.641 --> 00:17:25.665
Let's go.

00:17:58.341 --> 00:17:59.365
They've erected a force field

00:17:59.409 --> 00:18:01.343
with a rotating phase
modulation.

00:18:01.378 --> 00:18:02.902
We will not be able
to disrupt it.

00:18:02.979 --> 00:18:04.276
Voyager to Captain Janeway.

00:18:04.314 --> 00:18:05.281
Go ahead.

00:18:05.315 --> 00:18:06.282
We've just picked up

00:18:06.316 --> 00:18:08.580
an alien ship
leaving the planet.

00:18:08.618 --> 00:18:09.585
Tractor them, Commander.

00:18:09.619 --> 00:18:10.586
Sorry, Captain.

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:11.712
They went into warp too quickly.

00:18:11.755 --> 00:18:12.813
Beam us back.

00:18:12.856 --> 00:18:15.324
As soon as we're aboard,
lay in a course for pursuit,

00:18:15.358 --> 00:18:16.552
maximum warp.

00:18:16.593 --> 00:18:17.992
Aye, Captain.

00:18:31.441 --> 00:18:33.432
Okay...

00:18:36.346 --> 00:18:39.406
the isotropic restraint
is activated.

00:18:39.449 --> 00:18:40.416
He won't be able to move

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:41.940
more than two microns
in any direction.

00:18:42.052 --> 00:18:44.350
One micron would be preferable,

00:18:44.387 --> 00:18:45.854
but I suppose it'll have to do.

00:18:45.889 --> 00:18:47.686
Initiating
the holographic emitter array.

00:18:51.094 --> 00:18:53.426
His cellular toxicity is rising.

00:18:53.463 --> 00:18:54.452
82 percent.

00:18:54.497 --> 00:18:56.624
86 percent.

00:18:56.666 --> 00:18:58.133
Computer, activate program
Neelix-1.

00:18:58.168 --> 00:18:59.567
Deactivate the infuser.

00:19:02.439 --> 00:19:04.236
It's off-line.

00:19:04.274 --> 00:19:05.571
Here we go.

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:08.907
90 percent.

00:19:09.012 --> 00:19:10.274
95 percent.

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:19.120
Toxicity returning to normal.

00:19:24.294 --> 00:19:26.558
Captain's Log, supplemental.

00:19:26.596 --> 00:19:29.224
We are pursuing the alien ship
from the moon.

00:19:29.266 --> 00:19:31.131
They are not responding
to hails,

00:19:31.167 --> 00:19:33.226
and it appears
that our two ships

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.101
are evenly matched for speed.

00:19:35.138 --> 00:19:36.901
Holographic lungs?

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:38.407
Yes.

00:19:38.441 --> 00:19:41.672
And they're working perfectly,
if I do say so myself.

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:46.449
Try to breathe normally.

00:19:46.582 --> 00:19:49.278
How long can I live like this?

00:19:49.318 --> 00:19:50.376
I don't know.

00:19:50.419 --> 00:19:52.944
This is an unprecedented
medical procedure.

00:19:53.055 --> 00:19:55.819
We will be closely monitoring
your condition.

00:19:55.858 --> 00:19:58.952
When do I get out
of this restraint?

00:19:59.028 --> 00:20:00.859
Unless Captain Janeway

00:20:00.896 --> 00:20:03.194
is successful in retrieving
your original lungs,

00:20:03.232 --> 00:20:04.358
you'll have to stay

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:06.800
in the isotropic restraint
indefinitely.

00:20:08.404 --> 00:20:10.634
Indefinitely?

00:20:10.673 --> 00:20:13.233
You mean I...

00:20:13.275 --> 00:20:15.766
I could be in here
the rest of my life?

00:20:17.346 --> 00:20:18.904
Yes.

00:20:18.948 --> 00:20:20.643
But we're trying
to find the aliens

00:20:20.683 --> 00:20:22.310
that did this to you.

00:20:22.351 --> 00:20:24.444
The Captain is doing
everything she can.

00:20:26.021 --> 00:20:28.216
Well...

00:20:28.257 --> 00:20:31.556
if I'm going to be
in here a while,

00:20:31.594 --> 00:20:35.394
now's as good a time
as any to tell you.

00:20:35.431 --> 00:20:39.697
Your ceiling is hideous.

00:20:45.708 --> 00:20:47.505
I didn't design the room.

00:20:47.543 --> 00:20:48.874
I just work here.

00:20:48.911 --> 00:20:51.436
Something with a bit of color
would help.

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:55.610
Maybe a nice tapestry
or a painting.

00:20:57.553 --> 00:20:59.919
Uh, could you dim the lights
a little?

00:20:59.955 --> 00:21:02.617
I'm a doctor, Mr. Neelix,
not a decorator.

00:21:02.658 --> 00:21:03.818
Perhaps you would be willing

00:21:03.859 --> 00:21:05.952
to accommodate his...

00:21:06.061 --> 00:21:07.722
aesthetic needs.

00:21:07.763 --> 00:21:09.856
And some music would be nice.

00:21:09.899 --> 00:21:13.164
Or better yet,
are you programmed to sing?

00:21:15.204 --> 00:21:16.296
Janeway to Paris.

00:21:16.338 --> 00:21:18.203
Please report to the Bridge.

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:19.764
I'm on my way, Captain.

00:21:19.808 --> 00:21:22.504
You know where I am
if you need me.

00:21:22.545 --> 00:21:23.807
Thanks for everything.

00:21:25.781 --> 00:21:27.408
Oh, fine.

00:21:27.449 --> 00:21:29.917
I have a little accident,

00:21:29.952 --> 00:21:34.389
I lose a pair of lungs,
and the next thing I know

00:21:34.423 --> 00:21:38.792
Paris is swooping in
like a Rectilian vulture.

00:21:38.827 --> 00:21:41.455
What are you talking about?

00:21:41.497 --> 00:21:44.227
Didn't you hear the way
that he said that?

00:21:44.266 --> 00:21:48.202
"I'll be on the Bridge
if you need me."

00:21:48.237 --> 00:21:51.695
"If you need me."

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:52.707
Neelix...

00:21:52.741 --> 00:21:53.708
Why doesn't he just

00:21:53.742 --> 00:21:55.369
pull the plug
on the holographic emitter,

00:21:55.411 --> 00:21:57.140
and get it over with,
get me out of the way,

00:21:57.179 --> 00:21:58.544
so he can have you
all to himself?

00:21:58.581 --> 00:22:00.776
Neelix, you're overreacting.

00:22:00.816 --> 00:22:02.545
Tom's been very supportive.

00:22:02.585 --> 00:22:06.180
Ah, so it's "Tom" now, is it?

00:22:06.221 --> 00:22:08.382
You really have
nothing to worry about.

00:22:08.424 --> 00:22:09.914
We're friends, that's all.

00:22:09.959 --> 00:22:12.655
It's not you I'm worried about.

00:22:12.695 --> 00:22:14.720
It's him.

00:22:14.763 --> 00:22:16.196
He's just...

00:22:16.231 --> 00:22:20.964
one big hormone
walking around the ship.

00:22:21.070 --> 00:22:24.801
Don't you see the way
that he looks at you?

00:22:26.542 --> 00:22:27.873
This is ridiculous.

00:22:27.910 --> 00:22:31.505
I am not going to argue with you

00:22:31.547 --> 00:22:35.176
about something that exists
only in your imagination.

00:22:36.919 --> 00:22:39.251
I know you, Neelix.

00:22:39.288 --> 00:22:43.088
And I know you're afraid.

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:47.987
I just want you to know
that no matter what happens,

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:49.588
I'll be here with you.

00:22:49.632 --> 00:22:53.796
Ke... Kes...

00:22:53.836 --> 00:22:57.033
I could be in here
a very long time.

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:00.970
You need to go on
with your life.

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:02.409
Don't worry about me.

00:23:02.444 --> 00:23:03.911
Neelix...

00:23:03.946 --> 00:23:06.073
You should just let me die.

00:23:06.115 --> 00:23:07.605
Stop that.

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:10.551
We're going
to get through this together.

00:23:10.586 --> 00:23:12.281
Visiting hours are over.

00:23:12.321 --> 00:23:14.789
He needs rest.

00:23:14.823 --> 00:23:16.688
When can I come back?

00:23:16.725 --> 00:23:20.456
1700 hours,
not a minute before.

00:23:20.496 --> 00:23:22.555
I'll see you then.

00:23:29.338 --> 00:23:31.772
Don't worry.
I'm not going to kiss you.

00:23:31.807 --> 00:23:33.775
I'm only adjusting
the restraint.

00:23:33.809 --> 00:23:36.869
I'll try to contain
my disappointment.

00:23:39.515 --> 00:23:41.745
Captain, we've completed
our diagnostic

00:23:41.784 --> 00:23:43.115
on the alien device.

00:23:43.152 --> 00:23:45.450
It appears to be
more than a weapon.

00:23:45.487 --> 00:23:47.785
It's also a very sophisticated
medical scanner

00:23:47.823 --> 00:23:49.586
and surgical instrument.

00:23:49.625 --> 00:23:50.819
From what we can tell,

00:23:50.859 --> 00:23:52.986
It uses a neural resonator
to stun the victim

00:23:53.095 --> 00:23:54.585
while a quantum imaging scanner

00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:57.155
begins a microcellular
analysis of the entire body.

00:23:57.199 --> 00:23:58.791
The amount of information
this thing can gather

00:23:58.834 --> 00:24:00.699
puts a tricorder to shame.

00:24:00.736 --> 00:24:01.828
You fire this at someone,

00:24:01.870 --> 00:24:03.565
you learn everything
about their anatomy,

00:24:03.605 --> 00:24:05.937
right down
to their DNA sequencing.

00:24:06.008 --> 00:24:08.033
So it sounds like
we're dealing with aliens

00:24:08.077 --> 00:24:10.477
who've developed a technology
specifically designed

00:24:10.512 --> 00:24:14.380
to locate and extract organs
from other beings. Why?

00:24:14.416 --> 00:24:15.440
Captain.

00:24:18.487 --> 00:24:20.318
The alien ship
has dropped out of warp.

00:24:20.355 --> 00:24:22.823
It's approaching
a large asteroid.

00:24:22.858 --> 00:24:24.189
On screen.

00:24:24.226 --> 00:24:26.717
It's entered the asteroid,
Captain.

00:24:26.762 --> 00:24:27.729
Hold position here.

00:24:27.763 --> 00:24:28.730
Mr. Kim?

00:24:28.764 --> 00:24:29.890
I can't scan the interior.

00:24:29.932 --> 00:24:33.129
The surface stratum
is made up of some sort

00:24:33.168 --> 00:24:34.567
of neutronium alloy.

00:24:34.603 --> 00:24:36.901
It doesn't look like
a natural formation.

00:24:36.939 --> 00:24:39.134
Are you saying someone
built this asteroid?

00:24:39.174 --> 00:24:40.163
It's possible.

00:24:40.209 --> 00:24:41.574
Any sign of weapons
or defense systems?

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:43.510
Negative.

00:24:43.545 --> 00:24:45.672
I think I've located
where the alien ship entered.

00:24:45.714 --> 00:24:48.512
There's an open crater
on the limb of the asteroid.

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:49.710
Let's see it.

00:24:53.522 --> 00:24:56.582
The ion trail
from the alien ship

00:24:56.625 --> 00:24:57.990
leads directly inside.

00:24:58.026 --> 00:25:00.722
How large is that crater?

00:25:00.763 --> 00:25:03.527
200 meters in diameter.

00:25:03.565 --> 00:25:05.055
Captain,

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:06.931
may I suggest
you consider carefully

00:25:07.002 --> 00:25:08.333
what you are about to do.

00:25:08.370 --> 00:25:10.395
How do you know
what I'm about to do?

00:25:10.439 --> 00:25:12.304
I could describe to you
in detail

00:25:12.341 --> 00:25:13.603
the psychological observations

00:25:13.642 --> 00:25:16.475
I've made about you
over the past four years,

00:25:16.512 --> 00:25:19.106
which lead me to conclude
you're about to take

00:25:19.148 --> 00:25:22.140
this ship inside the asteroid,
but suffice it to say

00:25:22.184 --> 00:25:24.243
I know you quite well.

00:25:24.286 --> 00:25:27.255
One of these days, I'm going
to surprise you, Tuvok,

00:25:27.289 --> 00:25:29.314
but not today.

00:25:29.358 --> 00:25:31.349
I've already considered
other options.

00:25:31.393 --> 00:25:33.520
If Neelix has any chance
of surviving,

00:25:33.562 --> 00:25:35.962
we have to act fast.

00:25:36.064 --> 00:25:37.531
Red Alert.

00:25:37.566 --> 00:25:39.625
Mr. Paris, lay in a course.

00:25:39.668 --> 00:25:41.135
Aye, Captain.

00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:42.967
Mr. Tuvok, maximum shields.

00:25:43.071 --> 00:25:44.629
Phasers at the ready.

00:26:01.456 --> 00:26:05.688
Captain, I'm reducing power
to the aft thrusters only.

00:26:05.727 --> 00:26:08.321
This passageway is
getting a little

00:26:08.363 --> 00:26:10.126
too narrow for my taste.

00:26:10.164 --> 00:26:12.758
Use your discretion, Mr. Paris.

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:14.665
Any sign of the vessel,
Lieutenant?

00:26:14.702 --> 00:26:16.533
We're still following
the ion trail,

00:26:16.571 --> 00:26:19.597
but electromagnetic interference
is limiting our sensor range.

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:22.074
I'm only able to scan
500 meters ahead of us.

00:26:22.110 --> 00:26:24.340
Are there any indications

00:26:24.379 --> 00:26:26.108
we're being scanned or probed,
Mr. Kim?

00:26:26.147 --> 00:26:27.808
Not yet.

00:26:36.624 --> 00:26:38.023
Doctor?

00:26:40.495 --> 00:26:42.963
Doctor,
I require your assistance.

00:26:43.031 --> 00:26:44.794
What is it, Mr. Neelix?

00:26:44.832 --> 00:26:46.959
Please, it's urgent.

00:26:47.035 --> 00:26:48.559
Yes?

00:26:48.603 --> 00:26:50.730
I have an itch.

00:26:51.706 --> 00:26:53.435
An itch?

00:26:54.976 --> 00:26:57.069
Just above my left eyebrow.

00:27:01.582 --> 00:27:02.571
A little higher.

00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:06.910
Oh, thank you.

00:27:07.021 --> 00:27:08.420
You're quite welcome.

00:27:08.456 --> 00:27:10.549
Doctor, wait, don't leave.

00:27:10.591 --> 00:27:11.819
I'm not leaving, Mr. Neelix.

00:27:11.859 --> 00:27:13.156
I'm simply going
back to work.

00:27:13.194 --> 00:27:15.128
I can't see you over there.

00:27:16.831 --> 00:27:19.231
I... I feel like I'm all alone.

00:27:19.267 --> 00:27:20.598
You are all alone.

00:27:20.635 --> 00:27:22.330
I'm a holographic projection...

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:25.305
a projection with a lot
of work to do, I might add.

00:27:27.742 --> 00:27:30.074
That's very amusing, Doctor.

00:27:30.111 --> 00:27:33.308
But... um...

00:27:33.347 --> 00:27:38.080
I'm really starting to feel
a little, uh... trapped in here.

00:27:38.119 --> 00:27:41.020
A little claustrophobic maybe.

00:27:42.723 --> 00:27:44.247
I'm not sure what to do.

00:27:44.292 --> 00:27:46.920
There's nothing you can do
except lie there and be quiet.

00:27:46.961 --> 00:27:49.259
I'm going to lie here

00:27:49.297 --> 00:27:53.256
for the rest of my life,
aren't I?

00:27:53.301 --> 00:27:56.498
Just staring up at the ceiling.

00:27:57.672 --> 00:28:00.664
I'm not sure I can take that.

00:28:00.708 --> 00:28:05.077
I really need to get out
of this restraint.

00:28:06.647 --> 00:28:09.013
Doctor, I want you to let me
out of this thing

00:28:09.050 --> 00:28:10.278
right now.

00:28:10.318 --> 00:28:12.582
You know that's impossible.

00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:14.087
I'm the patient.

00:28:14.122 --> 00:28:15.350
I know what my rights are.

00:28:15.389 --> 00:28:20.088
I want to be released
from this restraint immediately.

00:28:21.662 --> 00:28:24.290
Immediately!

00:28:24.332 --> 00:28:28.860
Mr. Neelix, you are alive,
you are breathing

00:28:28.903 --> 00:28:30.632
and for the moment,
your condition is stable.

00:28:30.671 --> 00:28:33.265
That is more than most people
can say in your situation.

00:28:33.307 --> 00:28:35.571
Now, it is critical
to your recovery

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:38.841
that you not subject yourself
to any additional stress.

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:40.404
Try to calm down.

00:28:40.448 --> 00:28:42.211
Don't tell me to calm down!

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:44.741
You're not the one trapped
in a restraining field with...

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:47.615
...holo-lungs.

00:28:47.655 --> 00:28:49.782
I don't think they're working.

00:28:49.824 --> 00:28:53.817
I can't... I can't
get enough oxygen.

00:28:53.861 --> 00:28:56.591
There's something wrong.

00:28:56.631 --> 00:28:58.826
You're hyperventilating.

00:28:58.866 --> 00:29:01.562
Try to take slow,
natural breaths.

00:29:01.602 --> 00:29:03.570
I can't.

00:29:05.573 --> 00:29:06.801
I'm dying.

00:29:06.841 --> 00:29:08.206
Let me out of here.

00:29:09.243 --> 00:29:10.574
Will you help me?

00:29:10.611 --> 00:29:12.442
Help me.

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:13.742
Help me!

00:29:33.367 --> 00:29:36.165
You're doing just fine,
Mr. Paris. Just fine.

00:29:36.204 --> 00:29:38.331
Thanks, Captain.

00:29:38.372 --> 00:29:41.205
Sensors detect
a large chamber ahead.

00:29:50.151 --> 00:29:52.278
What the hell?

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:54.550
We appear to be seeing
Voyager and the alien ship

00:29:54.589 --> 00:29:56.284
reflecting off the walls
of the chamber.

00:29:56.324 --> 00:29:59.760
Can you determine which ship
is the real one?

00:29:59.794 --> 00:30:01.193
No, Captain.

00:30:01.229 --> 00:30:04.562
The walls are emanating severe
electromagnetic interference.

00:30:04.599 --> 00:30:06.123
I cannot scan them directly.

00:30:06.167 --> 00:30:08.658
It's like trying to move
through a hall of mirrors.

00:30:08.703 --> 00:30:11.968
You never know when you're
going to walk into the glass.

00:30:12.073 --> 00:30:14.769
I'm still picking up
the alien's ion trail.

00:30:14.809 --> 00:30:16.333
Maybe we should follow that.

00:30:16.377 --> 00:30:19.540
They might have left a fake
ion trail to lure us in here.

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:21.980
It could lead us right
into one of those walls.

00:30:22.016 --> 00:30:23.847
That's a chance we're
going to have to take.

00:30:23.884 --> 00:30:27.752
Tuvok, extend the deflectors
to maximum range.

00:30:27.788 --> 00:30:29.085
If we do run into something,

00:30:29.123 --> 00:30:30.920
it'll give us
an extra margin of error.

00:30:30.958 --> 00:30:33.620
Follow the ion trail,
Mr. Paris, slowly.

00:30:33.661 --> 00:30:35.856
Mr. Kim, continuous scans.

00:30:35.896 --> 00:30:37.830
Aye, Captain.

00:30:45.106 --> 00:30:48.098
I'll do what I can
to reassure him, Doctor.

00:30:48.142 --> 00:30:49.871
Good. If you don't mind,

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:51.104
I'd like you
to remain in Sick Bay,

00:30:51.145 --> 00:30:53.272
so you're here when he wakes up.

00:30:53.314 --> 00:30:54.906
Of course.

00:30:56.217 --> 00:30:57.275
Are you all right?

00:30:57.318 --> 00:30:58.342
Me?

00:30:58.386 --> 00:31:00.650
You seem agitated
about something.

00:31:00.688 --> 00:31:01.746
Of course I'm agitated.

00:31:01.789 --> 00:31:03.950
This entire situation
is getting out of control.

00:31:04.058 --> 00:31:05.355
You mean Neelix.

00:31:08.896 --> 00:31:11.126
First they tell me
there's no doctor,

00:31:11.165 --> 00:31:13.326
so I have to be
on call 24 hours a day.

00:31:13.367 --> 00:31:15.426
And then they tell me
there are no nurses,

00:31:15.469 --> 00:31:16.959
so I have no one to assist me.

00:31:17.038 --> 00:31:19.598
I thought Tom Paris
was assigned to you.

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:23.599
Like I said, no one
to assist me,

00:31:23.644 --> 00:31:27.740
and now I have a patient with
severe and possibly long-term

00:31:27.782 --> 00:31:30.512
emotional problems and there's
no Counselor on board.

00:31:32.219 --> 00:31:35.347
I am an emergency medical
supplement...

00:31:35.389 --> 00:31:37.524
a supplement, that's all.

00:31:37.657 --> 00:31:39.181
I wasn't programmed
for any of this.

00:31:39.225 --> 00:31:40.988
It's just...

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:43.494
unacceptable.

00:31:43.530 --> 00:31:46.693
I don't know anything about
holographic engineering,

00:31:46.733 --> 00:31:49.930
but if you want my opinion,
you haven't been acceptable.

00:31:49.969 --> 00:31:52.267
You've been remarkable.

00:31:52.305 --> 00:31:56.435
I've only done what
the program allows me to.

00:31:56.476 --> 00:31:58.671
Give yourself some credit.

00:31:58.711 --> 00:32:01.976
You saved his life.

00:32:02.015 --> 00:32:05.075
You did... not some program.

00:32:05.118 --> 00:32:08.451
It may seem that way to you.

00:32:08.488 --> 00:32:10.513
That's exactly how it seems.

00:32:12.792 --> 00:32:14.851
You're very...

00:32:14.894 --> 00:32:16.589
kind.

00:32:16.629 --> 00:32:17.960
How does a real doctor learn

00:32:18.031 --> 00:32:20.499
to deal with patients'
emotional problems, anyway?

00:32:20.533 --> 00:32:22.899
They learn from experience.

00:32:22.936 --> 00:32:25.063
Aren't you capable of learning?

00:32:25.104 --> 00:32:27.664
I have the capacity
to accumulate

00:32:27.707 --> 00:32:29.231
and process data, yes.

00:32:29.275 --> 00:32:33.336
Then I guess
you'll just have to learn...

00:32:33.379 --> 00:32:35.677
like the rest of us.

00:32:38.251 --> 00:32:40.719
Have you ever considered
a career in medicine?

00:32:42.722 --> 00:32:44.747
Neelix is starting
to regain consciousness.

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:49.259
Lieutenant, I'm picking up

00:32:49.295 --> 00:32:51.763
a minor power fluctuation
in the warp core.

00:32:51.798 --> 00:32:53.993
Compensate
with the KLS stabilizer.

00:32:54.033 --> 00:32:55.967
No effect.

00:32:56.002 --> 00:32:57.936
In fact, the power loss
is starting to accelerate.

00:32:57.971 --> 00:33:00.405
Janeway to Torres.
What's going on down there?

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:03.102
Some kind of power drain.

00:33:05.011 --> 00:33:07.502
I can't localize it, but...

00:33:07.547 --> 00:33:08.639
we're losing power

00:33:08.681 --> 00:33:10.342
at a rate of seven percent
per minute.

00:33:10.383 --> 00:33:12.874
The power drain is coming

00:33:12.919 --> 00:33:14.716
from somewhere in this chamber,
Captain.

00:33:14.754 --> 00:33:17.348
Some kind of dampening field.

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:20.223
It's bleeding energy directly
from the warp nacelles.

00:33:20.259 --> 00:33:22.090
B'Elanna,
shut down the warp core.

00:33:22.128 --> 00:33:24.153
Go to emergency power.

00:33:24.197 --> 00:33:26.097
No effect, Captain.

00:33:26.132 --> 00:33:27.599
Keep me informed.

00:33:27.634 --> 00:33:28.692
Bridge out.

00:33:28.735 --> 00:33:30.896
Can you pinpoint the source
of the dampening field?

00:33:30.937 --> 00:33:35.067
It appears to be coming
from 217 mark 015.

00:33:35.108 --> 00:33:37.099
Distance 547 meters.

00:33:37.143 --> 00:33:38.508
Tuvok.

00:33:38.544 --> 00:33:39.511
What would happen

00:33:39.545 --> 00:33:41.479
if we locked phasers
and fired at the source?

00:33:41.514 --> 00:33:44.381
The walls of this chamber
reflect directed energy.

00:33:44.417 --> 00:33:47.215
The phaser beam would ricochet
along an unpredictable path,

00:33:47.253 --> 00:33:49.483
possibly impacting
our ship in the process.

00:33:49.522 --> 00:33:52.218
All right.
We won't try that.

00:33:52.258 --> 00:33:53.555
Well, maybe we should.

00:33:53.593 --> 00:33:55.561
Tuvok, what would happen

00:33:55.595 --> 00:33:58.723
if we reduced the phaser
power level to a minimum setting

00:33:58.765 --> 00:34:00.665
and sent out
a continuous beam?

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:03.692
The phaser would continue
to reflect off the bulkheads

00:34:03.736 --> 00:34:06.204
until it encountered
a nonreflective material.

00:34:06.239 --> 00:34:08.833
Until it encountered
a nonreflective material...

00:34:08.875 --> 00:34:10.502
like the real alien ship.

00:34:10.543 --> 00:34:13.205
So we could use the phasers
like a searchlight...

00:34:13.246 --> 00:34:15.544
scan the interior of the station
until we find the ship.

00:34:15.581 --> 00:34:17.071
Exactly.

00:34:17.116 --> 00:34:18.447
Do it.

00:34:27.126 --> 00:34:29.117
Adjusting phaser azimuth
to 15 degrees.

00:34:29.162 --> 00:34:30.322
Wait a minute.

00:34:30.363 --> 00:34:31.660
I think I've found
the real ship.

00:34:31.698 --> 00:34:33.529
On screen.

00:34:36.869 --> 00:34:39.804
Mr. Paris, bring us within
transporter range of that ship.

00:34:39.839 --> 00:34:40.806
Aye, Captain.

00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:42.705
I'm picking up two life signs.

00:34:42.742 --> 00:34:44.073
They're powering engines.

00:34:44.110 --> 00:34:45.543
Bridge to Transporter Room 3.

00:34:45.578 --> 00:34:47.978
Lock on to those two life signs
and beam them aboard.

00:34:48.081 --> 00:34:49.673
Security, meet me
in Transporter Room 3.

00:34:49.716 --> 00:34:51.513
Aye, sir.

00:35:13.873 --> 00:35:16.341
You're on the Starship Voyager.

00:35:16.375 --> 00:35:18.240
I'm Captain Kathryn Janeway

00:35:18.277 --> 00:35:20.768
of the United Federation
of Planets.

00:35:20.813 --> 00:35:23.043
I am Dereth...

00:35:23.082 --> 00:35:26.449
of the Vidiian Sodality.

00:35:26.486 --> 00:35:28.351
You attacked
one of our crew members

00:35:28.387 --> 00:35:31.584
and you lured us
into this asteroid. Why?

00:35:31.624 --> 00:35:32.886
We are gathering

00:35:32.925 --> 00:35:37.919
replacement organs
and suitable biomatter.

00:35:37.964 --> 00:35:42.458
It is the only way we have
to fight the phage.

00:35:42.502 --> 00:35:43.799
A virus?

00:35:43.836 --> 00:35:45.861
Some kind of disease?

00:35:45.905 --> 00:35:46.963
Yes.

00:35:47.073 --> 00:35:50.975
It attacked our people
over two millennia ago.

00:35:51.043 --> 00:35:53.739
It consumes our bodies

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:57.910
destroys our genetic codes
and cellular structures.

00:36:00.219 --> 00:36:02.915
So you harvest the bodies
of other beings

00:36:02.989 --> 00:36:05.287
to replace your own tissues

00:36:05.324 --> 00:36:08.691
as they're consumed
by this... phage?

00:36:08.728 --> 00:36:12.061
Our immunotechnology
cannot keep up.

00:36:12.098 --> 00:36:14.566
The phage adapts.

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:16.090
It resists all attempts

00:36:16.135 --> 00:36:17.762
to destroy it.

00:36:17.804 --> 00:36:20.830
Our society has been ravaged.

00:36:20.873 --> 00:36:23.603
Thousands die each day.

00:36:23.643 --> 00:36:28.239
There is no other way
for us to survive.

00:36:30.883 --> 00:36:33.181
I have a great...

00:36:33.219 --> 00:36:36.814
sympathy for what
your race has endured.

00:36:36.856 --> 00:36:39.586
But I cannot allow you
to keep the organs you removed

00:36:39.625 --> 00:36:41.320
from one of our crew members.

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:43.555
We need them back immediately.

00:36:46.599 --> 00:36:51.400
I'm afraid that isn't possible.

00:36:51.437 --> 00:36:54.429
I have already
biochemically altered

00:36:54.473 --> 00:36:57.340
the air-breathing organs

00:36:57.376 --> 00:37:02.177
and grafted them
into Motura's body.

00:37:02.215 --> 00:37:05.241
They are a part of him now.

00:37:09.388 --> 00:37:11.788
He is my honatta.

00:37:11.824 --> 00:37:17.057
His task is to find
the organs I need for survival.

00:37:17.096 --> 00:37:19.428
We...

00:37:19.465 --> 00:37:23.902
we try to extract them
from the dead...

00:37:23.936 --> 00:37:28.430
But sometimes,
when the need is immediate

00:37:28.474 --> 00:37:32.774
more aggressive actions
are required.

00:37:32.812 --> 00:37:36.771
So now I am left with
the same choice you made...

00:37:36.816 --> 00:37:40.616
whether to commit murder
to save a life,

00:37:40.653 --> 00:37:45.090
or to allow my own crewman
to die

00:37:45.124 --> 00:37:48.719
while you breathe air
through his lungs.

00:37:50.963 --> 00:37:55.627
It must be impossible
for you to understand

00:37:55.668 --> 00:38:02.733
how any civilized people
could come to... this.

00:38:04.343 --> 00:38:07.005
Before the phage began,

00:38:07.046 --> 00:38:10.812
we were known as educators
and explorers...

00:38:10.850 --> 00:38:15.378
a people whose greatest
achievements were artistic.

00:38:15.421 --> 00:38:19.653
I, myself, am a sculptor
of note on my world.

00:38:19.692 --> 00:38:26.495
All I can say is that when your
entire existence is at stake...

00:38:26.532 --> 00:38:31.265
You don't have to explain
yourself, Motura.

00:38:34.106 --> 00:38:39.703
If the consequence of this act
is a death sentence,

00:38:39.745 --> 00:38:41.838
so be it.

00:38:41.881 --> 00:38:45.044
At least it will put
an end to my suffering.

00:38:48.354 --> 00:38:52.256
I can't begin to understand what
your people have gone through.

00:38:52.291 --> 00:38:55.419
They may have found a way
to ignore the moral implications

00:38:55.461 --> 00:38:58.794
of what you're doing,
but I have no such luxury.

00:38:58.831 --> 00:39:02.733
I don't have the freedom
to kill you to save another.

00:39:02.768 --> 00:39:05.794
My culture finds that
to be a reprehensible

00:39:05.838 --> 00:39:08.500
and entirely unacceptable act.

00:39:08.541 --> 00:39:09.872
If we were closer to home,

00:39:09.909 --> 00:39:13.242
I would lock you up and turn you
over to my authorities for trial

00:39:13.279 --> 00:39:15.372
but I don't even have
that ability here,

00:39:15.414 --> 00:39:18.440
and I am not prepared to carry
you forever in our brig.

00:39:20.586 --> 00:39:23.180
So I see no other alternative...

00:39:26.692 --> 00:39:28.827
but to let you go.

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:31.656
Take a message to your people.

00:39:31.696 --> 00:39:34.859
If I ever encounter
your kind again,

00:39:34.899 --> 00:39:38.630
I will do whatever is necessary
to protect my people

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:42.299
from this...
harvesting of yours.

00:39:42.340 --> 00:39:45.332
Any aggressive actions
against this ship

00:39:45.376 --> 00:39:49.608
or its crew will be met
by the deadliest force.

00:39:49.647 --> 00:39:52.582
Is that clear?

00:39:54.886 --> 00:39:56.911
Uh... quite.

00:39:58.423 --> 00:39:59.890
Wait.

00:39:59.924 --> 00:40:03.951
I want to see
this crewman of yours.

00:40:04.028 --> 00:40:06.963
That can serve
no useful purpose.

00:40:07.065 --> 00:40:09.158
Maybe it can.

00:40:09.200 --> 00:40:12.363
Our medical technologies
may be superior to theirs.

00:40:12.403 --> 00:40:15.463
I can tell you, from what
we've observed of them,

00:40:15.506 --> 00:40:18.498
that they are
considerably superior.

00:40:19.677 --> 00:40:22.373
She spared my life...

00:40:22.413 --> 00:40:24.472
our lives.

00:40:24.515 --> 00:40:26.142
We owe it to them to see

00:40:26.184 --> 00:40:29.176
if there's anything we
can do to help him.

00:40:31.556 --> 00:40:32.716
I'll take you to him.

00:40:45.536 --> 00:40:50.235
His simulated organs
are primitive.

00:40:50.275 --> 00:40:53.904
It's amazing he is still alive.

00:40:58.449 --> 00:41:03.216
This is set to scan only.

00:41:03.254 --> 00:41:04.721
Proceed...

00:41:04.756 --> 00:41:06.314
carefully.

00:41:28.680 --> 00:41:30.671
Strange.

00:41:30.715 --> 00:41:34.845
According to my readings,
you are not here.

00:41:34.886 --> 00:41:38.413
Believe me, I wish I weren't.

00:41:42.927 --> 00:41:45.987
The rest of you are healthy.

00:41:46.030 --> 00:41:49.625
You are compatible
for organ transplant.

00:41:49.667 --> 00:41:52.500
Wait a minute.
We've already considered this.

00:41:52.537 --> 00:41:54.266
Talaxian physiology
is different

00:41:54.305 --> 00:41:56.432
from that of anyone else
on this ship.

00:41:56.474 --> 00:41:59.534
His immune system would
reject their lungs immediately.

00:41:59.577 --> 00:42:03.377
Your surgical knowledge
is inferior.

00:42:03.414 --> 00:42:07.612
We will simply adapt
his immunogenicity.

00:42:07.652 --> 00:42:09.745
It won't be a problem.

00:42:09.787 --> 00:42:15.225
Which of you will donate a lung?

00:42:15.259 --> 00:42:16.226
Me.

00:42:16.260 --> 00:42:17.659
No!

00:42:17.695 --> 00:42:19.822
Absolutely not.
It's too dangerous.

00:42:19.864 --> 00:42:21.126
Let someone else do it.

00:42:21.165 --> 00:42:22.928
No, I want to do it, Neelix.

00:42:22.967 --> 00:42:24.730
I'm willing to take the risk.

00:42:24.769 --> 00:42:27.465
Besides, you've done
so much for me.

00:42:27.505 --> 00:42:29.871
Let me give you something once.

00:42:29.907 --> 00:42:32.432
Just for once.

00:42:34.712 --> 00:42:36.475
All right.

00:42:43.988 --> 00:42:45.580
I look forward

00:42:45.623 --> 00:42:48.387
to sampling your cooking,
Mr. Neelix.

00:42:48.426 --> 00:42:52.522
You mean I can keep my kitchen?

00:42:52.563 --> 00:42:57.364
Well, at least until we get
the replicators back on line.

00:42:57.402 --> 00:42:59.131
Thank you, Captain.

00:42:59.170 --> 00:43:01.468
And, Captain...

00:43:01.506 --> 00:43:03.565
I'll see you at breakfast.

00:43:10.214 --> 00:43:12.444
Captain's Log, supplemental.

00:43:12.483 --> 00:43:14.246
The aliens have successfully
transplanted

00:43:14.285 --> 00:43:16.446
one of Kes's lungs into Neelix.

00:43:16.487 --> 00:43:18.387
The dampening field
has been deactivated,

00:43:18.423 --> 00:43:21.051
and I have allowed them
to beam back to their vessel.

00:43:21.092 --> 00:43:22.582
We are resuming a course home.

00:43:34.138 --> 00:43:35.662
It's all right.

00:43:35.706 --> 00:43:38.266
The operation was a success.

00:43:38.309 --> 00:43:41.710
Neelix is asleep
and breathing on his own now...

00:43:41.746 --> 00:43:43.475
with your lung.

00:43:44.482 --> 00:43:46.848
I feel a little light-headed.

00:43:46.884 --> 00:43:48.181
That will pass.

00:43:48.219 --> 00:43:50.983
You'll soon adapt
to diminished lung capacity.

00:43:51.055 --> 00:43:54.081
I spoke to the Captain
about you.

00:43:54.125 --> 00:43:55.251
She's given me permission

00:43:55.293 --> 00:43:57.318
to begin training you
as a medical assistant.

00:43:57.361 --> 00:43:59.727
You'll be a backup for Mr. Paris

00:43:59.764 --> 00:44:03.325
or, possibly, a replacement.

00:44:03.367 --> 00:44:06.928
That is, assuming
you're interested.

00:44:06.971 --> 00:44:09.201
Of course I'm interested.

00:44:09.240 --> 00:44:11.208
When do we start?

00:44:11.242 --> 00:44:15.042
We'll begin your lessons as soon
as you've fully recovered.

00:44:17.482 --> 00:44:19.211
Thank you, Doctor.

00:44:21.119 --> 00:44:22.950
Thank you.

00:44:23.054 --> 00:44:27.150
You've... given me
a lot to think about.
